msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:692
msgid "Passkeys also need the site to run on https (browsers only allow them in a secure context)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:694
msgid "Install"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:695
msgid "In wp-admin, open <strong>Plugins → Add New</strong>, search for “Authlify”, then click <strong>Install Now</strong> and <strong>Activate</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:696
msgid "Or upload the plugin zip under <strong>Plugins → Add New → Upload Plugin</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:697
msgid "Or with WP-CLI:"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:699
msgid "What activation changes"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:700
msgid "On a new site, nothing about how people log in changes yet: the login page stays at wp-login.php until you choose a new address. Brute-force lockouts are on by default. Two-factor login is available but each person turns it on for themselves. CAPTCHA, the breached-password check and the hardening options are off until you switch them on."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:701
msgid "Authlify creates three database tables (activity log, lockout counters and passkeys) and a daily clean-up task."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:702
