msgid "Login URL"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:862
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1051
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:1300
msgid "How it works"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:863
msgid "It only sends requests to your own site’s address, logged out, without following redirects and without ever submitting a password. Each request carries a signed header, so the probes are not written to the activity log."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:864
msgid "It checks that the login address works and is not cached, then tries about forty routes: wp-login.php and disguised variants, every login action (register, lost password, logout and others), /wp-admin/ pages, the /login, /admin and /dashboard shortcuts, old Modify Login back doors, and public pages such as the homepage, a post, a 404 page, robots.txt, the sitemap, the feed and the REST index."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:865
msgid "A route leaks if it shows the login form, or mentions the address in a redirect or in the page."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:866
msgid "It also reports, as warnings, things that help attackers without revealing the address: XML-RPC multi-password requests, usernames in the REST API, and <code>?author=</code> scans."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:868
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1081
msgid "When it runs"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:869
msgid "About ten seconds after the login address or hiding settings change."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:870
msgid "Once a week, on the daily maintenance task."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:871
msgid "When you click <strong>Run Leak Check</strong> (at most once every 30 seconds), or run the WP-CLI command."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:872
msgid "It does nothing while no custom login address is set."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:874
msgid "Reading the result"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:874
#: inc/Diagnostics/LeakCheck.php:1330
msgid "Passed"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:874
msgid "No route revealed the login address. Warnings about XML-RPC, REST usernames or author scans can still be listed; fix them on the Hardening tab."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:875
msgid "Failed"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:875
msgid "At least one route revealed the address. Each result names the route and how to fix it. A theme or another plugin that prints wp_login_url() on public pages is the usual cause."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:876
msgid "Incomplete or untested"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:876
msgid "Your server could not reach itself (a “loopback” request). Some hosts block this; Site Health reports the same problem."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:877
msgid "Leak Check tests known routes on your own site. It cannot see links to your login address posted elsewhere, and it does not test pages behind a login."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:892
msgid "Authlify → Security → Brute force"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:893
msgid "Authlify → Security → Hardening"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:896
msgid "Lock out addresses that keep guessing passwords, with lockouts that grow longer for repeat offenders."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:898
msgid "A password-guessing script tries thousands of passwords. Limiting failed attempts per IP address makes that impossible, while everyone else keeps logging in as normal."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:901
msgid "<strong>Brute-force protection</strong>: on by default on new sites."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:902
msgid "<strong>Failed attempts allowed</strong> (default 5) within the <strong>Time window</strong> (default 15 minutes), per IP address."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:903
msgid "<strong>Lockout length</strong> (default 15 minutes)."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:904
msgid "<strong>Escalating lockouts</strong> (on by default): repeat lockouts last 1×, 4×, 16× and then 96× the lockout length. With the default 15 minutes that is 15 minutes, 1 hour, 4 hours, then 24 hours. The count starts again once an address has had no lockout for 24 hours."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:905
msgid "<strong>Network lockouts</strong> (off by default): when addresses from one network (an IPv4 /24 or IPv6 /64) fail three times the allowed attempts in total, the whole network is locked. It stops attackers who rotate addresses, but it can also lock out an office or a mobile carrier, so add your own address to “Never lock out” first."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:906
msgid "<strong>Targeted-account threshold</strong> (default 10): when one username collects this many failures from different addresses, the account is not locked (that would lock the real owner out). Instead, if a CAPTCHA is set to “Only after failed logins”, the login form asks for it for that username."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:909
msgid "When one or two attempts remain, the error says so. (This hint is hidden when “Login error messages” is on.)"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:910
msgid "A locked-out visitor sees “Too many failed login attempts. Please try again in N minutes”, with a link to email themselves an unlock link."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:911
msgid "The lock applies to the address, for every account, administrators included. People already logged in are not affected."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:912
msgid "Logins through XML-RPC, the REST API and application passwords are counted and locked too."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:913
msgid "A successful login clears that address’s failure count, but not an active lockout."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:914
msgid "Current lockouts are listed under <strong>Locked out right now</strong>, with <strong>Unlock</strong> and <strong>Unlock everyone</strong>. Unlocking an address also clears its network."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:915
msgid "Old counters are cleaned up daily."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:918
msgid "Lockouts only work if Authlify sees each visitor’s real IP address. Behind Cloudflare or a proxy, set the visitor IP option first."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:919
msgid "The allow and block lists only apply while brute-force protection is on."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:920
msgid "On multisite the counters and lockouts are shared by the whole network."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:922
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:1026
#, php-format
msgid "Related: %1$s, %2$s."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:922
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:926
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:97
msgid "Visitor IP address"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:922
#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:943
#: inc/Admin/ProtectionPage.php:116
msgid "Allow and block lists"
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:926
msgid "Tell Authlify how visitors reach your site, so lockouts hit the attacker and not your CDN."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:928
msgid "Behind Cloudflare, a load balancer or a reverse proxy, every request seems to come from the proxy. If Authlify locked that address, it would lock out everyone. Forwarding headers carry the real address, but anyone can fake them, so Authlify only reads them from proxies it can trust."
msgstr ""

#: inc/Admin/Docs.php:929
#, php-format
msgid "%s, panel <strong>Visitor IP address</strong>."
